Here is an example demonstrating the new platinum appearance views that have been introduced to MacApp Release 13.  Instructions for building the Grayscale Appearance MacApp example application.------------1. Open the .prj file with CodeWarrior.2. Select the Run command to compile, link, and launch the application.Overview of the Grayscale Appearance MacApp adapter classes.------------Standard Controls: TButton, TCheckBox, TRadio, TPopupPlatinum appearance integrated with MacApp standard controls.Icon Buttons:TIconPushButtonMA, TIconCheckBox, TIconRadio, TIconPopupReplacement subclass standard controls, and act like their counterparts, but with icon button appearance.TSliderA subclass of TControl. Implements AGA slider by instantiating a core CSlider_AC. If the fUserArea contains a valid STR# resource ID, the slider will be labeled/pointy, the slider labels are read from that resource, the slider range is initialized to match the number of labels. Otherwise, the slider will be unlabeled/rectangular.Minimum dimensions: Xx17 or 17xY.TBackgroundAdornerA subclass of TAdorner. To get a gray window background, attach this adorner to the window in front of the Draw adorner. This adorner is for a modeless window, such as a document window. For modal dialogs, see TFlatBackgroundBehavior and TModalBackgroundAdorner.TFlatBackgroundBehaviorA subclass of TBehavior. This behavior installs a gray background 'wctb' color in the window to which it is attached, so that the background is gray even in the structure region border of movableDBoxProc and dBoxProc procID windows. For other window procIDs, or if you prefer the white structure region border, use TModalBackgroundAdorner instead.TModalBackgroundAdornerA subclass of TAdorner. To get a gray dialog background, attach this adorner to the dialog in front of the Draw adorner. This adorner is for a modal window, such as a modal dialog box or movable modal dialog window. However, this adorner simply draws in the window content region, so you will get a white border in the structure region of movableDBoxProc and dBoxProc procID windows. If you want to avoid the white structure region border in these window types, use TFlatBackgroundBehavior instead.TWhiteBackgroundAdornerA subclass of TAdorner. To get a white subview background against a gray window background, attach this adorner to the subview in front of the Draw adorner. This adorner will be common for editable text views and list views, usually used with a TBorderFrameAdorner.TNotchedWhiteBackgroundAdornerA subclass of TWhiteBackgroundAdorner. It avoids painting white in the "notch" in the bottom right corner of the view.TBorderFrameAdornerA subclass of TAdorner. To get a "3D" sunken subview frame, attach this adorner to the subview behind the Draw adorner. This adorner will be common for editable text views and list views, usually used with a TWhiteBackgroundAdorner.TNotchedBorderFrameAdornerA subclass of TBorderFrameAdorner. It contours the border frame around the "notch" in the bottom right corner of the view.TStaticTextPlatinum Appearance support integrated in TStaticText. Implements static text that draws disabled in "real" gray (not qd.gray erase patBic), and erases to gray on a non-white background, by instantiating a core CStaticText_AC.TUpDownArrowA subclass of TControl. Implements AGA little arrows by instantiating a core CUpDownArrow_AC. If the fUserArea contains the subview ID of a TNumberText subview in the same window, the little arrows will automatically increment/decrement the value of the number field.TDisclosureTriangleA subclass of TControl. Implements AGA disclosure triangle by instantiating a core CDisclosureTriangle_AC.TProgressIndicatorA subclass of TCtlMgr. Implements AGA determinate and indeterminate progress indicators by instantiating a core CProgress indicator. To set the indicator's min/max range, call GetAGAControl()->SetRange. If you set min=max, you get an indeterminate indicator. Call GetAGAControl()->SetValue or Increment to prod a determinate indicator. For an indeterminate indicator, either set the fUserArea to non-zero to make the indicator automatically start idling with quantized animation, or you can call AGAProgressIndicatorMA::StartAutoAnimate yourself.Required dimensions: Xx14.TSeparatorA subclass of TView. Implements AGA separator by calling AGA DrawSeparator primitive. The larger dimension (h/v) is the direction that the separator is drawn.TTargetBorderViewReplacement subclass of TTargetBorderView. Implements AGA keyboard focus frame by changing the color and shape of the target border.TNotchedTargetBorderViewReplacement subclass of TTargetBorderViewe (TTargetBorderView). Same as TTargetBorderView, but contoured to wrap around the "notch" in the bottom right corner of the view.TTargetBorderFrameViewReplacement subclass of TTargetBorderView (TTargetBorderView). In addition to the AGA keyboard focus indicator, draws a "3D" sunken subview frame exactly like the TBorderFrameAdorner. This is so that you don't have to also attach an TBorderFrameAdorner to the subview.Recommended dimensions: n/a; fit to contain subview.TNotchedTargetBorderFrameViewReplacement subclass of TTargetBorderFrameView (TTargetBorderView). Same as TTargetBorderFrameView, but contoured to wrap around the "notch" in the bottom right corner of the view.TClusterPlatinum Appearance integrated with TCluster. Implements AGA primary group box by instantiating a core CCluster_AC set for "primary" look. Differs from TCluster in that if it has no title it still draws, so if you want a non-visible cluster, just use a TCluster. If the fUserArea contains the subview ID of a sibling subview, that other view's width will be used to determine the "title gap" that is left blank for the other view to occupy.If you use a sibling view for the title gap, you'll need to locate it at the correct location to sit in the title gap. As an example, if you use a 16xY check box for the group box title, the check box view should be located at left+14, top-9 relative to the group box. To conform precisely to the AGA guidelines, the title's baseline should match the group box top, and there should be 3 blank pixels on each side of the title.TSecondaryGroupBoxReplacement subclass of TCluster. Implements AGA secondary group box by instantiating a core CSecondaryCluster_AC set for "secondary" look.TTabbedViewA subclass of TControl. Implements AGA tab panels by instantiating a core CTabbedView_AC set for large tab format, and handling panel content switching. If the fUserArea contains a valid STR# resource ID, the number of tabs and their labels are initialized from the STR# resource; otherwise, the object is initialized with zero tabs. To assign a panel to an existing tab, call InstallPanel. To add a new panel and a new tab, call AddPanel. Panels are built from 'View' resources that are TView-based rather than TWindow-based.If it's sized larger than the size of the tabs alone, the frame box will be drawn and the tab panels will be switched in and out. If it's sized not much larger than the size of the tabs alone, there will be no frame box and no panel views will be switched in and out; the tabs will be drawn and you can catch the switching to do whatever you want with some other content view.TSmallTabbedViewReplacement subclass of TTabbedView, set for small tab format.Instructions for using the Platinum Appearance MacApp adapter classes.------------1. Set qGrayscaleAppearance to 1 in your ACS Core AutoSwitches_AC.h file.The Gray Council .cpp and .h files don't rely on any particular folder hierarchy, so you can organize them however you want.2. Initializing.First you should register all of the MacApp adapter classes that you use.3. Specifying the view class.(Note: feel free to open the file MyApplication.rsrc with AdLib to see the details of the view resources used for the example app.)For resource-based views, you just need to set the class name of the subview in the resource. For most views, this is all you need to do; in some of the fancier classes, you will also want to set attributes at runtime. *** THIS WILL CHANGE SHORTLY: Some classes also support use of the "user area" field to specify extra attributes ***. You can also statically instantiate views at runtime.- The easiest way to create new views with AGA objects is to install the Appearance AdLib palette extensions. Drag the custom palette prefs files (located in ":source:MacApp:AdLib extensions") into the "AdLib for MacApp Preferences" folder (located in the system preferences folder). When you launch AdLib, you'll now have extra palettes available for easily dragging appearance views into your windows. Just drag an object from the palette to your view layout window! (Note: This is supported for AdLib 2.6 or later.)- If you're not using the palette extensions--or if you are modifying existing views to make them grayscale--simply go to the subview's class name field and type in the new class name. That's all! Make sure you use the MacApp adapter class name (starting with a "T"), not the core ACS class name!- For an existing view in a derezzed View in a .r file, you type the Appearanc MacApp adapter class name between the "" double quotes near the top of the subview's definition.4. *** THIS WILL CHANGE SHORTLY: Setting the fUserArea ***.The following summarizes how the "user area" or "user data" field of a subview in a resource specifies additional information for certain classes (zero means to ignore it). This information is also listed in the class overviews above.TIconButton::fUserArea specifies the resource ID of an icon family for the icon button.TIconCheckBox::fUserArea specifies the resource ID of an icon family for the icon button.TIconRadio::fUserArea specifies the resource ID of an icon family for the icon button.TIconPopup::fUserArea specifies the resource ID of an icon family for the icon button.TSlider::fUserArea specifies the STR# resource ID of the slider labels.TUpDownArrow::fUserArea specifies the IDType of a TNumberText subview in the same window to be automatically linked to the arrows.TCluster::fUserArea specifies the IDType of a sibling subview whose width will be used to determine the "title gap" of the group box.TProgressIndicator::fUserArea, for indeterminate progress indicators (min==max), if non-zero, starts the indicator idling automatically.TTabbedView::fUserArea specifies the STR# resource ID of the tab labels. You still need to call InstallPanel to assign a panel to a tab in this case. If you're not using the fUserArea to init the tabs & labels, you will probably use AddPanel instead of InstallPanel.5. Changing attributes at runtime.Once you have a Gray Council view instantiated, either from a view resource or statically, you will want to do something with it! For the MacApp replacement subclasses, you can probably just plug them in; all standard functions like TCheckBox::Toggle and TPopup::GetCurrentItem will continue to work for the replacement subclasses. The only thing you need to avoid (and you should already be avoiding this) is for TCtlMgr subclasses, don't set or get the control manager value directly. You can continue to use the Set/GetLongMax functions, and you'll still be OK.For some classes, there is no MacApp equivalent, so you'll need to call the view's GetAGAControl()-> functions directly.  There is really only one thing to be careful of: focusing. To keep the core AGA classes as lightweight as possible, they do not store a GrafPtr. They assume that the port is set up by the caller. So you must make sure that the view is focused before you explicitly call an AGAControl function that will cause drawing. The MacApp adapter classes already do this by calling this->Focus() where necessary.The example application has an extra menu that tests the use of the standard MacApp dialogs.Modification History--------------------06/06/97	bpa		Initial notes purloined from Gray Council MacApp notes.